<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">

    <section class="section" id="archive">
      <h2><span class="num">01 — Context</span>The Archive That No One Read</h2>
      <p>In the NEOWISE Single-Exposure Database, 188,876,840,852 rows of infrared photometry — every detection the spacecraft recorded across 10.5 years of sweeping the sky — sat queryable and public for over a decade. Almost no one had systematically searched them for objects that change.</p>
      <p>The scale is worth pausing on. The database logs approximately 450 million distinct light sources. Each source has multiple detections — "apparitions" — captured as the spacecraft orbited, re-scanning the same patch of sky every six months. The result is a time-series archive of the infrared sky: not a single snapshot but a decade-long record of how bright each source was every time the telescope passed over it. This is the raw material for studying variability — sources whose brightness changes over time. Pulsating stars, eclipsing binaries, erupting young stellar objects, active galactic nuclei, supernovae. Variability is not exotic. It is one of the primary ways astronomers learn what objects are and how they behave.</p>
      <p>The data was never hidden. It was hosted at NASA's Infrared Processing and Analysis Center at Caltech, freely downloadable, open to anyone with a query. The bottleneck was not access. It was attention. As the VARnet paper put it directly: "There has been a lack of progress in studying the WISE database in its raw form (single-exposure photometry). Its size, in the hundreds of billions of rows, is no doubt a limiting factor."</p>
      <p>Other projects tried to make the data tractable. UnWISE and unTimely stacked exposures to improve image depth and reduce noise. Both are valuable. Both deliberately eliminated or degraded the time-domain information — the very dimension needed to detect variability. They made the data smaller. They did not make anyone faster at reading it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="origin">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — Origin</span>The Right Question</h2>
      <p>Davy Kirkpatrick is an IPAC senior scientist at Caltech, a Steward Observatory PhD alumnus who grew up in a farming community in Tennessee. His ninth-grade chemistry and physics teacher, Marilyn Morrison, showed him what mentorship could do. He has mentored high school students for five consecutive summers, along with undergraduates, citizen scientists, and visiting graduate fellows. "I wanted to pass on that same sort of mentoring to someone else," he told Caltech, "and hopefully many someone elses."</p>
      <p>Matteo Paz arrived at Kirkpatrick's door through a specific sequence. In grade school, he attended Caltech's Stargazing Lectures — public events on campus that sparked an interest in astronomy. In the summer of 2022, he joined Caltech's Planet Finder Academy, a program led by Professor of Astronomy Andrew Howard. The following summer, in 2023, he enrolled in Caltech's six-week Summer Research Connection and was paired with Kirkpatrick as his mentor.</p>
      <p>The pairing produced a question. Kirkpatrick proposed the research direction: could anyone systematically analyze the NEOWISE single-exposure data to identify variable objects? Paz wanted to do more than a summer project. He wanted to publish a paper. "I'm so lucky to have met Davy," Paz said. "He didn't discourage me. He said, OK, so let's talk about that."</p>
      <p>What followed was not a single burst of work but a sustained collaboration. "Every meeting with Davy is 10 percent work and 90 percent us just chatting," Paz said. "It's been super cool just to have someone to talk to about science like that." Kirkpatrick's orientation toward mentoring was direct: "If I see their potential, I want to make sure that they are reaching it. I'll do whatever I can to help them out." According to the Caltech press release, Kirkpatrick also connected Paz with other Caltech astronomers — Shoubaneh Hemmati, Daniel Masters, Ashish Mahabal, and Matthew Graham — to deepen his expertise. By 2024, Paz was employed at IPAC as a paid staff researcher under NASA funding while still attending Pasadena High School.</p>
      <p>The technical question took shape: can a model classify the variability signature of every source in the NEOWISE single-exposure database fast enough to make a full-sky survey feasible?</p>
    </section>

    <div class="phantom-wrap">
      <canvas class="varnet-pasadena-student-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Apparition</strong> — A dense field of near-invisible points rests on warm cream; a staggered wave of recognition sweeps left to right and fourteen points brighten to burnt orange, each pulsating at its own period; two or three stutter and fade back to the undifferentiated field; the remaining sources dim and withdraw; nothing arrives, nothing leaves.</p>

    <section class="section" id="instrument">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Instrument</span>VARnet</h2>
      <p>VARnet is the model. A single-author paper — Matthew Paz, published in <em>The Astronomical Journal</em> (AJ 168, 241, December 2024) — describing a proof of concept, not a catalog. The paper's own abstract frames it explicitly: "a proof of concept for both the efficacy of VARnet and methods for an upcoming variability survey over the entirety of the NEOWISE dataset."</p>
      <p>The architecture is a nine-layer convolutional neural network with two distinctive features. The first is a wavelet decomposition step: after three initial convolutional layers, the model applies a biorthogonal 2.2 discrete wavelet transform to the signal, concatenates the wavelet coefficients with the original input, and passes both through three more convolutional layers. This gives the network access to both the raw light curve and its frequency structure simultaneously. The second is a custom module called the Finite-Embedding Fourier Transform, or FEFT. NEOWISE light curves are not all the same length — some sources have dozens of detections, others have hundreds, depending on sky position and observation cadence. The standard discrete Fourier transform produces variable-length outputs from variable-length inputs. FEFT replaces the square DFT matrix with a rectangular one, projecting every input sequence into a fixed-dimension embedding regardless of length. After the FEFT module, three final convolutional layers feed into three fully connected layers and a softmax classifier.</p>
      <p>Paz trained VARnet on 1.06 million synthetic light curves — not real data, but signals generated from mathematical models of four variability classes: Null (non-variable), Transient (sudden brightness changes with decay), Pulsating (periodic or quasi-periodic intrinsic variability), and Transit (eclipsing binary systems). He validated the model on 231 real known variable objects. The result: an F1 score of 0.914 across all four classes, with accuracy of 92.2%. In binary mode — variable versus not variable — the F1 rose to 0.974.</p>
      <p>The speed is the point. VARnet processes a single source in under 53 microseconds on an NVIDIA Quadro RTX 6000 GPU with 22 GB of VRAM. For the 25-square-degree pilot region around the Orion Nebula — 62,841,525 rows of data — the model evaluation step took 0.41 seconds. The preprocessing step, a DBSCAN clustering algorithm that groups individual detections into coherent light curves for each source, took 287 seconds for the same region. Classification is not the bottleneck. Data preparation is.</p>

