      <p class="vps-lc-footer">Both sources have the same noise level. The signal is hiding in the scatter — VARnet picks it out in under 53 microseconds.</p>

      <p>Even in the 25-square-degree pilot, the model produced results. It confirmed the known variable system V1403 Ori, identified a new eclipsing binary, detected a potential supernova in galaxy LEDA 358365, and found a feeding supermassive black hole in LEDA 340305. All from a patch of sky that had been photographed, archived, and publicly available for years. The objects were there. They had not been classified.</p>
      <p>Paz estimated that at VARnet's classification speed, a full-sky model evaluation would take approximately 20.5 minutes. The proof of concept worked. The question was what would happen at scale.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="survey">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — The Survey</span>VarWISE</h2>
      <p>VarWISE is the catalog — a different paper, a different scope, a different authorship. Paz et al. 2026, published in <em>The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series</em> (ApJS 284, 41, June 2026). Five authors: Matthew Paz, J. Davy Kirkpatrick, Rajiv Uttamchandani, Troy Raen, and Roc M. Cutri, all affiliated with IPAC at Caltech. This was collaborative work.</p>
      <p>The full-sky application processed approximately 199.7 billion rows from the NEOWISE Single-Exposure Source Table, examining over 450 million distinct light sources across the infrared sky. The pipeline ran in two stages. VARnet performed the first-pass morphological triage, sorting every source into one of three categories — Null, Transient, or Continuous Variable (note the shift from four classes to three, collapsing the original distinctions for the coarser first pass). A separate XGBoost classifier, trained on 910,697 known variable objects and achieving an F1 of 0.95, then sorted all non-null sources into nine astrophysical classes: Cepheids, RR Lyrae, long-period variables, cataclysmic variables, Algol-type eclipsing binaries, W Ursae Majoris-type eclipsing binaries, young stellar objects, active galactic nuclei, and supernovae.</p>
      <p>The result: two catalogs. The Extended Catalog contains 1,918,082 objects. The Pure Catalog — a high-confidence subset — contains 457,080. Of the Extended Catalog's objects, 82.02% had no prior designation as a variable source or active galactic nucleus in SIMBAD, the astronomical reference database. That amounts to approximately 1,573,000 objects.</p>
      <p>A critical clarification, because nearly all press coverage of this work got it wrong: "new" does not mean "never before observed." Every one of these objects was already in the NEOWISE database. They had been detected by the spacecraft, recorded as rows in a public archive, and available for anyone to examine. What they lacked was a classification. No one had labeled them as variable. They were observed but not attended to. "New" means newly noticed — not newly seen. The distinction matters, and the story exists partly because the press coverage collapsed it.</p>

