          <div class="vps-cat-row"><span class="vps-cat-label">Amplitude</span><span class="vps-cat-value">0.38 mag</span></div>
          <div class="vps-cat-row"><span class="vps-cat-label">Period</span><span class="vps-cat-value">0.6214 d</span></div>
          <div class="vps-cat-row"><span class="vps-cat-label">Modulation</span><span class="vps-cat-value">O'Connell effect, 14–18 day timescale</span></div>
          <div class="vps-cat-row"><span class="vps-cat-label">SIMBAD match</span><span class="vps-cat-value highlight">No prior variable designation</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="vps-cat-count">1 of 1,918,082</div>
        <div class="vps-cat-footer">One entry. One object sitting in the archive all along, observed but never classified. The catalog holds 1.9 million more.</div>
      </div>

      <p>VarWISE J180414.35+675412.5, shown above, is one of the catalog's specific discoveries: an eclipsing binary exhibiting the O'Connell effect — an asymmetry in brightness between the two maxima of its light curve — with a 14-to-18-day modulation timescale. The catalog also reports VarWISE J141848.45−232439.6, another eclipsing binary, along with Cepheid variables demonstrating Hertzsprung progression in their light curve morphology, and young stellar object candidates associated with nebulosity and star-forming regions.</p>
      <p>Both catalogs are publicly hosted at NASA/IPAC's Infrared Science Archive with permanent DOIs: 10.26131/IRSA656 for the Pure Catalog, 10.26131/IRSA657 for the Extended Catalog, and 10.26131/IRSA658 for the associations table. The data is accessible via IRSA's Catalog Search Tool and API. The science product is real, downloadable, and verifiable.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="integrity">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — Integrity</span>The Honest Ledger</h2>
      <p>The VarWISE authors did something that none of the press coverage about their work did: they told the reader exactly where the catalog is wrong.</p>
      <p>Section 4.3 of the paper presents a class-by-class spot-check of the automated classifications. The numbers are not uniformly flattering. Of 212 objects classified as supernovae, only 18 — 9% — are solid supernova candidates. A majority, 56%, are likely normal AGN activity misidentified by the classifier. Of 149 objects classified as cataclysmic variables, 92% are actually young stellar object outbursts — genuinely variable sources, but mislabeled. The model found real change. It named the change wrong.</p>

      <div class="vps-ql-panel">
        <div class="vps-ql-header">
          <span class="vps-ql-dot"></span>
          VarWISE Quality Assessment — Authors' Own Disclosure
        </div>
        <div class="vps-ql-body">
          <table class="vps-ql-table">
            <caption>Class-by-class spot-check of VarWISE automated classifications, as reported by the authors.</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Class</th>
                <th scope="col">Checked</th>
                <th scope="col">Solid</th>
                <th scope="col">Primary Contamination</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span class="vps-ql-class">SN</span></th>
                <td>212</td>
                <td>18 (9%)</td>
                <td><span class="vps-ql-contam">56% likely normal AGN activity</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span class="vps-ql-class">CV</span></th>
                <td>149</td>
                <td>12 (8%)</td>
                <td><span class="vps-ql-contam">92% actually YSO outbursts</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span class="vps-ql-class">AGN</span></th>
                <td>84</td>
                <td>71 (85%)</td>
                <td><span class="vps-ql-contam">15% possibly unresolved galaxies</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span class="vps-ql-class">CEP</span></th>
                <td>229</td>
                <td>187 (~82%)</td>
                <td><span class="vps-ql-contam">18% periods match WISE 6-month cadence</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span class="vps-ql-class">LPV</span></th>
                <td>81</td>
                <td>77 (95%)</td>
                <td><span class="vps-ql-contam">5% false positives (nebular edges)</span></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="vps-ql-note">CV "contamination" is instructive: those 137 objects are still genuine variables — the model detected real change but assigned the wrong astrophysical label.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vps-ql-footer">Source: Paz et al. 2026, ApJS 284, 41 — Section 4.3</div>
      </div>

      <p>The Cepheid class carries its own kind of ambiguity: roughly 18% of candidate periods align with NEOWISE's six-month observation cadence, meaning the instrument's own rhythm may be producing phantom periodicities. The long-period variable class is the cleanest, with a 5% false positive rate (4 of 81 spot-checked), three of those four caused by nebular edge processing artifacts. No trade press article covering this work mentioned any of these figures.</p>
      <p>The paper states the constraint plainly: "automated classification is only as reliable as the data on which it was trained" and "the diversity and quality of prior training data remain the principal constraint for infrared-variability studies." A catalog with acknowledged imperfections and published DOIs is infinitely more useful than a headline number no one can check. The contamination disclosures are not a weakness of the catalog. They are what distinguish a peer-reviewed science product from a press release.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="recognition">
      <h2><span class="num">06 — Recognition</span>First Place</h2>
      <p>On March 11, 2025, at the National Building Museum in Washington, D.C., Matteo Paz won the $250,000 first-place prize in the 84th Regeneron Science Talent Search. The competition distributed over $1.8 million to 40 finalists, with $3.1 million in total awards including school bonuses. Pasadena High School received $2,000.</p>
      <p>His STS entry was titled "VarWISE — the First Complete Infrared Variability Survey — 1.9M Variables Classified into 10 Classes." The project name confirms what the judges evaluated: not the VARnet model paper alone, but the catalog work. The Society for Science described Paz as having found "1.5 million new potential objects" — the word "potential" is significant. Even the competition organizer used qualifying language.</p>
      <p>A timeline gap is worth noting. The STS prize was awarded in March 2025. The VARnet model paper had been published in December 2024 — single-author, peer-reviewed. But the VarWISE catalog paper, the five-author work that reports the 1.9 million objects and the contamination analysis, was not submitted until May 2026 and published in June 2026, more than a year after the prize. The STS judges evaluated unpublished catalog work. The published science that supports the competition claims arrived later.</p>
      <p>"I was just happy to have had the privilege," Paz told Forbes. "Not only placing in the top ten, but winning first place, came as a visceral surprise." Kirkpatrick's reaction was less measured: "When they announced Matteo was the winner of the science competition, that was the highest high I've ever had."</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="signal">
      <h2><span class="num">07 — Signal</span>The Attention Deficit</h2>
      <p>The lesson of VARnet and VarWISE is not that a teenager did something impressive. It is that a public archive of 189 billion data points contained over a million unclassified variable objects, and the bottleneck was never the telescope, the database, or the access policy. It was the absence of a tool fast enough to pay attention at the scale the data demanded.</p>
      <p>The paper's own observation makes this concrete. Existing projects that tried to make the NEOWISE data tractable — unWISE, unTimely — degraded the time-domain information. They made the data smaller so humans could manage it. VARnet took a different approach. It did not reduce the data. It made the reader faster. That is a different category of solution: not compression but attention.</p>
      <p>Paz identified the generalizability himself: "The model I implemented can be used for other time domain studies in astronomy, and potentially anything else that comes in a temporal format." The VarWISE paper names the remaining constraint: "the diversity and quality of prior training data remain the principal constraint for infrared-variability studies." The limit is not compute. It is not data. It is not access. It is the quality of the questions we teach the instrument to ask. And some of those questions are producing answers no one anticipated. "What's exciting," Paz told Forbes, "is that some variable phenomena I'm detecting don't have obvious explanations."</p>
      <p>The bottleneck in discovery has moved. It is no longer collection. It is classification. Not observation but attention. Public archives across science — not just astronomy — hold more data than anyone has instruments of attention for. VARnet did not build a better telescope. It built a faster pair of eyes. The question it leaves is how many other archives sit in the same state: data acquired, attention unaffordable, discoveries waiting inside noise that no one has yet asked the right question of.</p>
    </section>

  </div>
</StoryLayout>

<script>
(function () {
  const canvas = document.querySelector('.varnet-pasadena-student-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement | null;
  if (!canvas) return;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  if (!ctx) return;

  /* --- Colors from computed style --- */
  const BG_COLOR = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--bg').trim() || '#f5f0e8';
  const STORY_RAW = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--story').trim() || '#e2703a';

  function hexToRgb(hex: string): [number, number, number] {
    const h = hex.replace('#', '');
    return [parseInt(h.slice(0, 2), 16), parseInt(h.slice(2, 4), 16), parseInt(h.slice(4, 6), 16)];
  }

  const [accR, accG, accB] = hexToRgb(STORY_RAW.startsWith('#') && STORY_RAW.length >= 7 ? STORY_RAW : '#e2703a');
  /* Warm grey for unresolved points — dark on cream, very low alpha */
  const DIM_R = 26, DIM_G = 26, DIM_B = 23;

  const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  let W = 0, H = 0, raf = 0, frame = 0;

  const ro = new ResizeObserver(() => {
    W = canvas.offsetWidth;
    H = canvas.offsetHeight;
    canvas.width = W * devicePixelRatio;
    canvas.height = H * devicePixelRatio;
    ctx.setTransform(devicePixelRatio, 0, 0, devicePixelRatio, 0, 0);
  });
  ro.observe(canvas);

  /* --- Phase thresholds (1200 frames = ~20s at 60fps) --- */
  const P1_END = 120;    /* Stillness */
  const P3_END = 960;    /* Hold */
  const P4_END = 1140;   /* Withdrawal */
  const CYCLE  = 1200;   /* Silence, then restart */
  const STUTTER_START = 720;
  const STUTTER_DUR   = 120;

  const NUM_PTS    = 65;
  const NUM_VAR    = 14;
  const NUM_CONTAM = 3;

  interface Pt {
    x: number; y: number;
    vx: number; vy: number;
    baseR: number;
    baseA: number;
    isVar: boolean;
    isContam: boolean;
    actFrame: number;
    period: number;
    isIrreg: boolean;
    dispA: number;
    dispR: number;
    mix: number;
  }

  const pts: Pt[] = [];

  function init() {
    pts.length = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < NUM_PTS; i++) {
      pts.push({
        x: 20 + Math.random() * Math.max(1, W - 40),
        y: 20 + Math.random() * Math.max(1, H - 40),
        vx: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.2,
        vy: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.2,
        baseR: 1.5 + Math.random() * 1.5,
        baseA: 0.06 + Math.random() * 0.04,
        isVar: false,
        isContam: false,
        actFrame: 0,
        period: 100,
        isIrreg: false,
        dispA: 0,
        dispR: 0,
        mix: 0,
      });
    }

    const idxs: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < NUM_PTS; i++) idxs.push(i);
    for (let i = idxs.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
      const j = (Math.random() * (i + 1)) | 0;
      const tmp = idxs[i]; idxs[i] = idxs[j]; idxs[j] = tmp;
    }
    const varIdxs = idxs.slice(0, NUM_VAR);
    varIdxs.sort((a, b) => pts[a].x - pts[b].x);

    for (let vi = 0; vi < varIdxs.length; vi++) {
      const p = pts[varIdxs[vi]];
      p.isVar = true;
      p.actFrame = P1_END + vi * 30;
      p.period = 60 + Math.random() * 180;
      p.isIrreg = vi < 2;
    }

    const contamCandidates = varIdxs.slice().sort((a, b) => pts[a].baseR - pts[b].baseR);
    for (let c = 0; c < NUM_CONTAM; c++) {
      pts[contamCandidates[c]].isContam = true;
    }

    for (const p of pts) {
      p.dispA = p.baseA;
      p.dispR = p.baseR;
      p.mix = 0;
    }
  }

  const pulOut = { a: 0, r: 0 };

  function pulsateInto(p: Pt, elapsed: number) {
    let ePeriod = p.period;
    if (p.isIrreg) {
      const cycleNum = Math.floor(elapsed / p.period);
      ePeriod = p.period * (1 + 0.2 * Math.sin(cycleNum * 2.7));
    }
    if (ePeriod < 10) ePeriod = 10;
    const phase = (elapsed % ePeriod) / ePeriod;
    const norm = 0.5 + 0.5 * Math.sin(phase * Math.PI * 2);
    pulOut.a = 0.5 + 0.4 * norm;
    pulOut.r = p.baseR + 1.5 * norm;
  }

  function update() {
    const cf = frame % CYCLE;

    /* Re-sort the wavefront at each cycle boundary */
    if (cf === 0 && frame > 0) {
      const varPts: Pt[] = [];
      for (const p of pts) { if (p.isVar) varPts.push(p); }
      varPts.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
      for (let vi = 0; vi < varPts.length; vi++) {
        varPts[vi].actFrame = P1_END + vi * 30;
      }
    }

    /* Brownian drift, ~0.1 px/frame */
    for (const p of pts) {
      p.vx += (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.02;
      p.vy += (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.02;
      p.vx *= 0.99;
      p.vy *= 0.99;
      const mv = 0.12;
      if (p.vx > mv) p.vx = mv;
      if (p.vx < -mv) p.vx = -mv;
      if (p.vy > mv) p.vy = mv;
      if (p.vy < -mv) p.vy = -mv;
      p.x += p.vx;
      p.y += p.vy;
      if (p.x < 8)     { p.x = 8;     p.vx = Math.abs(p.vx); }
      if (p.x > W - 8) { p.x = W - 8; p.vx = -Math.abs(p.vx); }
      if (p.y < 8)     { p.y = 8;     p.vy = Math.abs(p.vy); }
      if (p.y > H - 8) { p.y = H - 8; p.vy = -Math.abs(p.vy); }
    }

    for (const p of pts) {
      p.dispA = p.baseA;
      p.dispR = p.baseR;
      p.mix = 0;

      if (!p.isVar) continue;
      if (cf < P1_END) continue;
      if (cf >= P4_END) continue;
      if (cf < p.actFrame) continue;

      const sinceAct = cf - p.actFrame;

      /* Contaminated sources: fully faded once the stutter completes */
      if (p.isContam && cf >= STUTTER_START + STUTTER_DUR) continue;

      /* Contaminated sources mid-stutter */
      if (p.isContam && cf >= STUTTER_START) {
        const se = cf - STUTTER_START;
        const t = se / STUTTER_DUR;

        let pmult: number;
        if (t < 0.33)      pmult = 0.5;
        else if (t < 0.66) pmult = 2.0;
        else               pmult = 0.5;

        const sp = Math.max(10, p.period * pmult);
        const phase = (se % sp) / sp;
        const norm = Math.max(0, 0.5 + 0.5 * Math.sin(phase * Math.PI * 2));

        const peakA = 0.9 * (1 - t * 0.9);
        p.dispA = p.baseA + (peakA - p.baseA) * norm;
        p.dispR = p.baseR + 1.5 * (1 - t) * norm;
        p.mix = Math.max(0, 1 - t);
        continue;
      }

      /* Phase 4: withdrawal */
      if (cf >= P3_END) {
        const wt = (cf - P3_END) / (P4_END - P3_END);
        pulsateInto(p, sinceAct - 40);
        const amp = 1 - wt;
        p.dispA = p.baseA + (pulOut.a - p.baseA) * amp;
        p.dispR = p.baseR + (pulOut.r - p.baseR) * amp;
        p.mix = 1 - wt;
        continue;
      }

      /* Brightening, then pulsating at the source's own period */
      if (sinceAct < 40) {
        const t = sinceAct / 40;
        p.mix = t;
        p.dispA = p.baseA + (0.8 - p.baseA) * t;
        p.dispR = p.baseR;
      } else {
        pulsateInto(p, sinceAct - 40);
        p.dispA = pulOut.a;
        p.dispR = pulOut.r;
        p.mix = 1;
      }
    }
  }

  function draw() {
    ctx.fillStyle = BG_COLOR;
    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);

    for (let i = 0; i < pts.length; i++) {
      const p = pts[i];

      const cr = Math.round(DIM_R + (accR - DIM_R) * p.mix);
      const cg = Math.round(DIM_G + (accG - DIM_G) * p.mix);
      const cb = Math.round(DIM_B + (accB - DIM_B) * p.mix);

      if (p.mix > 0.3 && p.dispA > 0.25) {
        const gr = p.dispR * 5;
        const grad = ctx.createRadialGradient(p.x, p.y, 0, p.x, p.y, gr);
        grad.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(' + accR + ',' + accG + ',' + accB + ',' + (p.dispA * 0.12).toFixed(4) + ')');
        grad.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(' + accR + ',' + accG + ',' + accB + ',0)');
        ctx.fillStyle = grad;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, gr, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }

      ctx.globalAlpha = p.dispA;
      ctx.fillStyle = 'rgb(' + cr + ',' + cg + ',' + cb + ')';
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, p.dispR, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
  }

  /* Reduced motion: a single frozen Phase-3 frame */
  function drawStatic() {
    for (const p of pts) {
      if (!p.isVar) {
        p.dispA = p.baseA;
        p.dispR = p.baseR;
        p.mix = 0;
      } else if (p.isContam) {
        p.mix = 0.35;
        p.dispA = 0.22;
        p.dispR = p.baseR + 0.4;
      } else {
        const seed = (p.baseR + p.period) * 0.37;
        const norm = 0.5 + 0.5 * Math.sin(seed);
        p.dispA = 0.5 + 0.4 * norm;
        p.dispR = p.baseR + 1.5 * norm;
        p.mix = 1;
      }
    }
    draw();
  }

  function tick() {
    frame++;
    update();
    draw();
    raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    init();
    if (prefersReduced) {
      drawStatic();
    } else {
      const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => {
        if (e.isIntersecting) { if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick); }
        else { cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; }
      }, { threshold: 0.01 });
      io.observe(canvas);

      window.addEventListener('pagehide', () => {
        cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
        ro.disconnect();
        io.disconnect();
      });
    }
  });
})();
</script>
